{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Use the goodwill, don't mistake it for a fix</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Visual polish is worth investing in, it's a real, measurable driver of how forgiving users are of small friction, first impressions, and overall satisfaction. Just don't let glowing qualitative feedback after a visual refresh stand in for checking whether an underlying interaction problem, a confusing flow, a missing loading state, a genuinely broken edge case, actually got fixed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      When evaluating a redesign, separate the two questions explicitly: did perception improve (surveys, sentiment, support-ticket tone) and did the task itself improve (completion rate, time on task, error rate, support ticket volume for that specific flow). A redesign can legitimately win on the first and be flat on the second, and that's useful information, not a contradiction, it tells you the visual work earned trust but a follow-up pass on the actual interaction is still owed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Be especially careful with this bias in usability testing itself: a moderated session testing a good-looking prototype can produce artificially generous think-aloud feedback and lower reported friction than the same flow will get once it's live at scale with real, high-stakes tasks and no researcher in the room being polite to. Where possible, pair subjective usability ratings (SUS-style surveys, which are themselves subject to this bias) with objective task metrics, and weight redesign decisions on the objective side when the two disagree.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

